// **What the iterator's ordering terminals demand of the element type.**
//
// The negative half of `ordering_terminals.noe`, and it is the *same* half the eager list
// reductions have: `min`/`max` order the elements, so they need what `<` needs. An iterator is not
// a loophole around that — the requirement is read from one table for both surfaces, so a pipeline
// cannot quietly order a type the eager spelling refuses.
//
// An unbounded type parameter is E0025 with a one-word fix; a concrete element type that carries no
// ordering is E0007 naming the element.
// expect: error E0025 at 16:42
// expect: error E0007 at 19:6
// expect: exit 1

struct Plain { b: int }

fn smallest<T>(xs: List<T>): ?T { return xs.iter().min(); }

parts = [Plain { b: 2 }, Plain { b: 1 }];
echo parts.iter().max();
echo smallest([1, 2]);
